================================================================================
 /etc/fstab FILE
================================================================================

1. OVERVIEW
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The /etc/fstab (file systems table) file is a system configuration file found 
on Linux and Unix-like operating systems. It normally resides in the /etc/ directory.

- Primary Purpose: To define how disk partitions, various other file systems, and 
  remote devices (like NFS shares or SMB mounts) should be integrated into the 
  file system tree during the boot process.

- Key Function: It translates human-readable mount requests into automated system 
  instructions, specifying what to mount, where to mount it, what options to 
  apply, and when to check the file system for errors.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. SYNTAX STRUCTURE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Each file system entry is placed on its own line. Blank lines are ignored, and 
lines starting with a hash (#) are treated as comments.

Format:
<file system>             <mount point>   <type>  <options>       <dump>  <pass>
UUID=a1b2c3d4-e5f6...     /               ext4    defaults        0       1

Field Breakdown:
1. File System (<file system>): Specifies the partition, device, or remote share 
   to be mounted. Best practice is to use the UUID (Universally Unique Identifier) 
   or LABEL to prevent boot failures if device names change.

2. Mount Point (<mount point>): The directory path where the file system will 
   be attached (e.g., /, /home, /mnt/data).

3. File System Type (<type>): The format of the file system (e.g., ext4, xfs, 
   btrfs, vfat, swap, nfs, iso9660).

4. Mount Options (<options>): Configuration flags separated by commas (no spaces). 
   Controls behavior like read/write permissions, security, and performance.

5. Dump (<dump>): Used by the dump utility for backups. Most modern systems set 
   this to 0 (disabled).

6. Pass (<pass>): Determines the order in which fsck (file system check) is run 
   at reboot.
   - 0: Do not check.
   - 1: Root file system (/)—should always be checked first.
   - 2: Other file systems checked after the root file system.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. COMMON MOUNT OPTIONS REFERENCE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- defaults: Applies default options (rw, suid, dev, exec, auto, nouser, async).

- ro / rw: Mounts the file system as Read-Only or Read-Write.

- noexec: Prevents the execution of binary files or scripts on this partition.

- nosuid: Disables SUID/SGID bits, preventing privilege escalation.

- nodev: Interprets character or block special devices incorrectly/prevents them.

- noatime: Stops updating inode access times on read (improves SSD performance).

- nofail: Prevents the boot process from hanging if the drive fails to mount.

- async / sync: Controls I/O behavior. async buffers writes, sync writes directly.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. PRACTICAL EXAMPLES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Example A: Standard Local SSD/HDD Partition (ext4)
UUID=4a8e2b10-9c3f-4d5e-8b1a-7f6e5d4c3b2a  /data  ext4  defaults,noatime  0  2

Example B: Secure Temporary Storage (/tmp hardening)
UUID=9f8e7d6c-5b4a-3f2e-1d0c-9b8a7f6e5d4c  /tmp   ext4  defaults,nosuid,nodev,noexec  0  2

Example C: External Drive or Cloud Volume with Fault Tolerance
UUID=3c2b1a0f-9e8d-7c6b-5a4f-3e2d1c0b9a8f  /mnt/external  ext4  defaults,nofail,noatime  0  2

Example D: Swap Space Partition
UUID=12345678-abcd-1234-abcd-123456789abc  none   swap  sw  0  0

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. PRO-TIPS & SAFETY BEST PRACTICES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Always use UUIDs: Run the command 'blkid' to find unique IDs instead of using volatile device names like /dev/sdb1.
- Test Before Rebooting: Always test your configuration using:  #sudo mount -a
  (If mount -a returns no errors, your configuration syntax is valid.)

- Keep Backups: Always copy your working fstab before making edits:  #sudo cp /etc/fstab /etc/fstab.bak
